{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Twin Star Rocket, the Rock Island's Minneapolis-Houston streamliner of 1945-1967, named for the Lone Star and North Star states. Drawn here from Saint Paul to Fort Worth, the part of its run that can be traced on real track. It came down the Rock Island's Spine Line through Albert Lea, Mason City and Des Moines, crossed to Topeka by way of Kansas City, and then ran the railroad's own Kansas-Texas main line through Wichita, Enid, El Reno and Duncan into Fort Worth -- the line drawn in rock_island_topeka_fort_worth.geojson. Its discontinuance in March 1967 ended Rock Island passenger service between Kansas and Texas.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_Star_Rocket", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Lea_Subdivision (the Albert Lea Subdivision is a segment of the former Chicago, Rock Island and Pacific \"Spine Line\" from the Twin Cities to Kansas City)", "https://www.kansashistory.gov/p/chicago-rock-island-and-pacific-railway-records/13753 (Kansas Historical Society) -- SEEN ONLY AS A SEARCH-ENGINE QUOTE: a contract of Mar 19 1887 gave the Chicago, Kansas and Nebraska joint use of Union Pacific track between Kansas City and North Topeka for 999 years.", "data/burlington_rock_island.geojson in this repository (the jointly owned Fort Worth - Houston line, drawn there)"], "not_yet_sourced": "NOT DRAWN, Minneapolis to Saint Paul: the train's northern terminal was the Milwaukee Road's Minneapolis depot, reached over other railroads' tracks; this feature starts at Saint Paul Union Depot. NOT DRAWN, Fort Worth to Houston: that leg ran over the Burlington-Rock Island, the line the Rock Island half-owned with the Burlington, already drawn in data/burlington_rock_island.geojson -- drawing it again here would double the same rails in the same colour. The 26 intermediate stops are not marked; the route is drawn as a line through the stations the source names. avg_speed_kmh is derived from the sourced end-to-end schedule, so it is an average including stops rather than a running speed. The identification of the Minnesota trackage as ex-Rock Island rests on the Wikipedia Albert Lea Subdivision article; OpenStreetMap carries no historical operator tag there, and the corridor also carried Minneapolis & St. Louis and Milwaukee Road lines, so that leg is the least certain part of the geometry.", "geometry_sources": ["Traced on real track in OpenStreetMap by corridor-constrained Dijkstra, simplified Douglas-Peucker at 40 m. Overpass API (maps.mail.ru mirror), bbox dumps pulled 2026-08-27, corridor waypoints from OSM place nodes for the stations the Twin Star Rocket route map names: Saint Paul, Northfield, Faribault, Owatonna, Albert Lea, Manly, Mason City, Iowa Falls, Des Moines, Carlisle, Chariton, Trenton, Cameron, Kansas City, Lawrence, Topeka, Herington, Wichita, Caldwell, Enid, El Reno, Chickasha, Duncan, Waurika, Fort Worth.", "Where a leg ran on Rock Island rails the graph was restricted to ways tagged old_railway_operator=CRI&P plus untagged track, with every way carrying another railroad's operator tag excluded. The Kansas City-Topeka leg was deliberately routed on the Union Pacific Kansas Subdivision instead, because that is whose track the train used.", "Leg lengths: Saint Paul-Des Moines 257.3 mi; Des Moines-Kansas City approach 209.6 mi (98% CRI&P-tagged); Kansas City-Topeka 69.3 mi (UP Kansas Subdivision); Topeka-Fort Worth 525.5 mi. Straight-line bridges: 3.01 km, 38.91 km and 0.52 km."], "pulled": "20260827"}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Chicago, Rock Island & Pacific Railroad", "line": "Chicago, Rock Island & Pacific: Twin Star Rocket", "id": "rock-island-twin-star-rocket", "color": "#D62839", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 85, "speed_source": "estimated from the sourced schedule: 1,363 miles in 25 hours 50 minutes southbound is about 53 mph / 85 km/h average including stops", "geometry_quality": "Real-traced, 1088.0 mi from Saint Paul to Fort Worth, against roughly 1,073 miles implied by the sourced figures (1,363 miles Minneapolis\u2013Houston, less about 280 for Fort Worth\u2013Houston and about 10 for Minneapolis\u2013Saint Paul) -- a 1.01x match. Assembled from four separately routed legs. Saint Paul\u2013Des Moines 257.3 mi on the ex-Rock Island Spine Line, today the Union Pacific's Albert Lea and Mason City Subdivisions (OpenStreetMap carries no old_railway_operator tag on the Minnesota half, so the Wikipedia identification of the Albert Lea Subdivision as ex-Rock Island is what places it). Des Moines\u2013Kansas City approach 209.6 mi, 98% on ways tagged old_railway_operator=CRI&P. Kansas City\u2013Topeka 69.3 mi on the Union Pacific's Kansas Subdivision -- which is correct rather than an error: the Rock Island never owned this stretch and ran it on UP trackage rights. Topeka\u2013Fort Worth 525.5 mi, the Rock Island's own line (see rock_island_topeka_fort_worth.geojson for its per-leg tag composition). THREE STRAIGHT-LINE APPROXIMATIONS, 3.01 km inside Des Moines and 0.52 km at Topeka where the legs snapped to different nodes, and one that matters: 38.91 km (24.2 mi) across the northern approach to Kansas City, where OpenStreetMap has no ex-Rock Island alignment at all -- forcing a route through there put the train down an abandoned interurban, so it is drawn as a straight line instead. That is 2.2% of the feature's length. Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 10 ruled segments totalling 169 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed).", "segments": [{"start": 1945, "end": 1964, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Twin Star Rocket entered service Jan 14 1945, the only new streamlined train the government allowed into service during the Second World War. It ran 1,363 miles from the Milwaukee Road's Minneapolis depot to Houston as trains 507 and 508 -- the second-longest north-south run under a single railroad in the country, and the longest measured by great circle distance -- in about 25 hours 50 minutes. Its equipment came from the Kansas City-Minneapolis Rocket and the Kansas City-Dallas Texas Rocket; sleepers were added at Kansas City for the overnight run south, and the diners came from the Golden State, named for cacti. Daily each way", "sources": ["Segment-level: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_Star_Rocket (introduced Jan 14 1945; the only new streamlined train permitted to enter service in World War II; Minneapolis to Houston, 1,363 miles; trains 507 and 508; 25 hours 50 minutes southbound and 25 hours 40 minutes northbound; daily; equipment drawn from the Kansas City-Minneapolis Rocket and the Kansas City-Dallas Texas Rocket; Cactus-series diners from the Golden State; fully streamlined in 1948). The 1440-minute headway encodes the sourced daily frequency.", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_Star_Rocket"]}, {"start": 1964, "end": 1967, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "In October 1964 the southern end was cut back from Houston to Fort Worth, leaving passengers to change to the Burlington-Rock Island's Sam Houston Zephyr -- which was itself cancelled in 1966. The Twin Star Rocket was discontinued in March 1967. The drawn line ends at Fort Worth throughout, so this cutback is described here rather than shown; the Fort Worth-Houston leg is a separate layer. Daily each way", "sources": ["Segment-level: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_Star_Rocket (\"In October 1964, the terminus was curtailed from Houston to Fort Worth, requiring passengers to transfer to the Sam Houston Zephyr. That train was cancelled in 1966. In March 1967, the Twin Star Rocket was discontinued.\"). data/burlington_rock_island.geojson in this repository (the jointly owned Fort Worth - Houston line, drawn there)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twin_Star_Rocket"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-97.88685, 36.2071], "to": [-97.90987, 36.0456], "chord_mi": 11.23, "traced_mi": 11.23, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Enid Subdivision (11.23 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-97.93189, 35.83175], "to": [-97.99166, 35.67774], "chord_mi": 11.11, "traced_mi": 11.16, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Enid Subdivision (11.16 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-97.79241, 36.70931], "to": [-97.86609, 36.42248], "chord_mi": 20.21, "traced_mi": 20.24, "snap_m": [0, 1], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Enid Subdivision (20.24 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-97.61162, 36.98628], "to": [-97.7853, 36.72764], "chord_mi": 20.14, "traced_mi": 20.29, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Enid Subdivision (20.29 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-97.12586, 37.99937], "to": [-97.32624, 37.72505], "chord_mi": 21.77, "traced_mi": 22.76, "snap_m": [1, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Lost Springs Subdivision (21.97 mi)", "Enid Subdivision (0.24 mi)", "UP Wichita Industrial Lead (0.23 mi)", "(rail) (0.18 mi)", "Lost Springs Subdivision (0.08 mi)", "Wichita Yard 001 (0.06 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-97.32932, 37.64913], "to": [-97.3802, 37.45471], "chord_mi": 13.73, "traced_mi": 13.72, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Enid Subdivision (13.72 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-93.39535, 42.31285], "to": [-93.39529, 42.03636], "chord_mi": 19.1, "traced_mi": 19.11, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Mason City Subdivision (19.11 mi)"], "method